/-!
# j(q^r) is not rational in j and the j(qᵖ), p∈ S

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.jqN_prime_not_mem_adjoin` (`80c9a2aa-2c5c-5aa9-ae39-f3296c43be03`),
by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `ec5f8305-42b1-5eb4-84b8-af2f2cfaa80d`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Work inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of rational Laurent series, realised as `LaurentSeries ℚ`.
Here `jq` denotes the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot j_{\mathrm{num}}$, where $j_{\mathrm{num}}$ is the
integral power series `jNum` with its coefficients mapped into $\mathbb{Q}$, so `jq` is the
$q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$; and for $N\neq 0$, `jqN N` is the image of `jq` under
the ring homomorphism `qExpand ℚ N`, which multiplies all exponents by $N$, i.e. the substitution
$q\mapsto q^{N}$, giving $j(q^{N})$. The data are: a finite set $S$ of natural numbers, a hypothesis
that every $p\in S$ is prime, and a further prime $r$ with $r\notin S$. The conclusion is that
$j(q^{r})$ does not lie in the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated
by the set consisting of `jq` together with all Laurent series of the form `jqN p` for $p\in S$
(each such $p$ being nonzero). Equivalently, $j(q^{r})\notin \mathbb{Q}\bigl(j(q),\,j(q^{p}):p\in
S\bigr)$.

This is the descent step in a purely algebraic treatment of the degree of the modular equation: the
successive extensions $\mathbb{Q}(j)\subseteq \mathbb{Q}(j,j(q^{p}):p\in S)$ are all proper at each
new prime, which is what makes the local degree computations $[\,F(j(q^{p})):F\,]=p+1=\psi(p)$
applicable. It is used in the computation of relative degrees for adjoining several primes and for
prime-square levels, and hence in identifying $[\mathbb{Q}(j)(j_N):\mathbb{Q}(j)]$ with $\psi(N)$,
i.e. the irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_N$ over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$.

